IVX INSTAGRAM-STYLE REGISTRATION RELIABILITY
PHASE 2 — HARDEN SIGNUP SO ONE FAILURE NEVER CRASHES OR LOSES THE USER

EXECUTE END TO END.
DO NOT REDESIGN THE FULL LANDING PAGE.
DO NOT ADD NEW BUSINESS FEATURES.
HARDEN ONLY REGISTRATION, LOGIN, SESSION CREATION, MEMBER CREATION, AND FLOW RECOVERY.

==================================================
OBJECTIVE
==================================================

Build a resilient registration flow similar to mature high-volume consumer applications.

The required behavior is:

- the form never crashes;
- one failed backend step does not crash the page;
- double taps do not create duplicate users;
- network loss does not lose entered information;
- users can resume safely;
- partial accounts are repaired automatically;
- every failure has a trace ID;
- passwords and tokens are never logged;
- the user receives a clear next action;
- the same registration works on mobile web, Android app, iPhone, and desktop.

Required flow:

FORM
→ VALIDATE
→ CREATE REQUEST ID
→ AUTH USER
→ MEMBER PROFILE
→ INVESTMENT INTEREST
→ SESSION
→ CONTINUE
→ VERIFY
→ COMPLETE

==================================================
1. ONE CANONICAL AUTH CONFIGURATION
==================================================

Create one shared configuration loader.

Required canonical values:

window.IVX_SUPABASE_URL
window.IVX_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY

or one equivalent documented configuration object.

All landing modules must import or read the same configuration:

- index.html
- ivx-invest.js
- ivx-portal.js
- registration
- login
- password recovery
- session restore

Do not allow separate variable names in different files.

At startup run:

validateAuthConfiguration()

It must verify:

- URL exists;
- URL is not a placeholder;
- anon key exists;
- project reference matches production;
- Supabase library loaded;
- client can be created;
- environment is production.

If invalid:

- disable signup;
- show controlled unavailable state;
- attach trace ID;
- do not throw an uncaught exception.

==================================================
2. REGISTRATION STATE MACHINE
==================================================

Implement one explicit state machine.

Allowed states:

IDLE
VALIDATING
SUBMITTING
AUTH_CREATING
PROFILE_CREATING
INTEREST_CREATING
SESSION_CREATING
EMAIL_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED
COMPLETED
RECOVERABLE_ERROR
BLOCKED
RATE_LIMITED

Do not use scattered booleans such as:

isLoading
isCreating
isDone
hasError

as the authoritative workflow.

Every transition must be legal and logged.

Example:

IDLE
→ VALIDATING
→ SUBMITTING
→ AUTH_CREATING
→ PROFILE_CREATING
→ INTEREST_CREATING
→ SESSION_CREATING
→ COMPLETED

If profile creation fails after Auth succeeds:

PROFILE_CREATING
→ RECOVERABLE_ERROR

not:

→ generic crash

==================================================
3. IDEMPOTENT SUBMISSION
==================================================

Every form submission must create:

registrationRequestId

Use:

crypto.randomUUID()

Send it with every backend call.

Backend must enforce:

one registrationRequestId
= one logical registration

Store:

- request ID;
- normalized email hash;
- auth user ID;
- member ID;
- opportunity ID;
- current stage;
- final status;
- timestamps.

If the user taps twice:

- second request returns the existing result;
- no duplicate Auth user;
- no duplicate member;
- no duplicate investment-interest row.

==================================================
4. PRESERVE THE USER’S FORM
==================================================

Before network submission, save non-sensitive pending form state locally:

- first name;
- last name;
- email;
- selected deal;
- selected amount;
- current step;
- registration request ID.

Never save:

- plaintext password;
- access token;
- refresh token;
- tax ID;
- identity document.

If the browser refreshes or crashes:

1. restore the pending registration context;
2. inspect server status using request ID;
3. continue from the correct step;
4. do not restart blindly.

==================================================
5. PASSWORD SAFETY
==================================================

Password rules:

- minimum 12 characters;
- allow symbols;
- allow passphrases;
- do not lowercase;
- do not rewrite characters;
- do not store locally;
- do not log;
- do not include in analytics;
- do not include in trace payloads.

After submission:

- keep it only in component memory;
- clear it after successful Auth creation;
- clear it when leaving the registration screen;
- never display it in screenshots or error reports.

==================================================
6. CLIENT-SIDE VALIDATION
==================================================

Validate before sending:

- first name present;
- last name present;
- valid email;
- password policy;
- terms accepted;
- selected opportunity exists;
- amount is within allowed range;
- no forbidden characters where relevant.

Validation errors must not call the backend.

Use inline field errors.

Do not use one generic alert for all validation failures.

==================================================
7. BACKEND ORCHESTRATION
==================================================

Use one backend registration orchestrator.

Endpoint example:

POST /api/members/register

Responsibilities:

1. Validate request schema.
2. Validate idempotency key.
3. Normalize email.
4. Inspect existing request.
5. Create or locate Auth user.
6. Create or repair member/profile.
7. Create or repair investment-interest row.
8. Return session instructions.
9. Return exact stage.
10. Persist final result.

Do not make the browser responsible for coordinating multiple privileged database writes.

==================================================
8. PARTIAL-FAILURE RECOVERY
==================================================

Handle every partial state.

CASE A:
Auth user exists, member missing.

Action:
- create missing member;
- continue;
- do not create second Auth user.

CASE B:
Auth and member exist, interest missing.

Action:
- create missing interest;
- continue.

CASE C:
Everything exists, session missing.

Action:
- sign in or require email confirmation;
- continue.

CASE D:
Duplicate email.

Action:
- return EMAIL_EXISTS;
- offer Log In and Forgot Password.

CASE E:
User exists but registration request is incomplete.

Action:
- reattach and resume.

==================================================
9. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS
==================================================

Add or verify unique constraints:

profiles.auth_user_id UNIQUE
members.auth_user_id UNIQUE
members.normalized_email UNIQUE where appropriate
landing_investments.registration_request_id UNIQUE

Use foreign keys:

member.auth_user_id → auth.users.id
investment_interest.member_id → members.id
investment_interest.opportunity_id → deals.id

Do not rely only on frontend duplicate prevention.

==================================================
10. SAFE RETRIES
==================================================

Retry automatically only for:

- network interruption;
- HTTP 502;
- HTTP 503;
- HTTP 504;
- temporary Supabase connectivity failure.

Use bounded exponential backoff:

attempt 1: immediate
attempt 2: 1 second
attempt 3: 3 seconds

Maximum: 3 attempts.

Do not automatically retry:

- invalid email;
- weak password;
- invalid credentials;
- email already exists;
- authorization failure;
- validation failure;
- rate limit;
- duplicate request already completed.

==================================================
11. RATE LIMITING
==================================================

Use Supabase and server rate limits.

Configure correct end-user IP forwarding when requests pass through a backend or proxy so all users are not incorrectly treated as one server IP. Supabase documents rate limits and forwarded-IP support for this purpose.

Required limits:

- registration per IP;
- registration per email;
- password reset per email;
- login attempts per device/IP;
- OTP requests;
- resend confirmation.

User-facing rate-limit message:

“Too many attempts. Please wait before trying again.”

Do not permanently lock the user out.

==================================================
12. CUSTOM SMTP
==================================================

Do not rely on the default Supabase email sender for production.

Configure owner-controlled SMTP for:

- email confirmation;
- password reset;
- magic link if used;
- invitation email.

Supabase’s default sender is intended for limited testing and has restrictive availability/rate limits; production should use custom SMTP. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

Verify:

- SPF;
- DKIM;
- DMARC;
- sender domain;
- allowed redirect URLs;
- deep links;
- email delivery logs;
- resend flow.

==================================================
13. ERROR BOUNDARIES
==================================================

Add a registration-specific error boundary.

It must catch:

- configuration errors;
- Supabase client initialization failure;
- JavaScript render errors;
- unexpected response shapes;
- session parsing errors.

A registration failure must not crash the entire landing page.

Fallback actions:

- Retry;
- Log In;
- Forgot Password;
- Contact Support;
- Continue Later.

==================================================
14. NORMALIZED ERROR FORMAT
==================================================

Backend must return:

{
  "ok": false,
  "code": "PROFILE_CREATION_FAILED",
  "message": "We could not finish creating your account.",
  "traceId": "...",
  "stage": "PROFILE_CREATING",
  "retryable": true
}

Supported codes:

INVALID_EMAIL
WEAK_PASSWORD
EMAIL_EXISTS
AUTH_CREATION_FAILED
PROFILE_CREATION_FAILED
INTEREST_CREATION_FAILED
SESSION_CREATION_FAILED
EMAIL_CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED
RATE_LIMITED
NETWORK_ERROR
SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE
CONFIGURATION_ERROR
UNKNOWN_ERROR

Do not return raw stack traces to visitors.

==================================================
15. CLIENT REQUEST TIMEOUTS
==================================================

Use a controlled timeout.

Recommended:

- configuration check: 3 seconds;
- normal registration request: 15 seconds;
- email confirmation polling: asynchronous;
- session refresh: 10 seconds.

A timeout must not create a second registration automatically.

After timeout:

1. query registration status by request ID;
2. determine whether the first request completed;
3. resume or retry safely.

==================================================
16. SESSION RELIABILITY
==================================================

After successful registration:

- persist session through the official Supabase client;
- refresh tokens automatically;
- validate session on page reload;
- load member profile;
- restore selected investment context;
- continue to Step 4.

React Native clients must also handle foreground/background transitions safely using AppState and refresh the session when the app becomes active. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

==================================================
17. USER EXPERIENCE
==================================================

Use clear progress:

Creating secure account…
Setting up your member profile…
Saving your opportunity interest…
Signing you in…

Do not leave the user on a frozen spinner.

Button behavior:

- disable immediately after press;
- show progress;
- allow cancellation only before Auth creation;
- re-enable only after controlled failure;
- never submit twice.

==================================================
18. OBSERVABILITY
==================================================

Record:

- trace ID;
- request ID;
- stage;
- duration;
- HTTP status;
- Supabase error code;
- auth result;
- member result;
- interest result;
- session result;
- device/browser;
- app/site version;
- commit SHA.

Do not record sensitive field values.

Build an owner dashboard metric:

- registrations started;
- registrations completed;
- completion rate;
- failure by stage;
- duplicate attempts;
- rate-limited attempts;
- average completion time;
- email-confirmation completion rate;
- abandoned registration count.

==================================================
19. HEALTH CHECKS
==================================================

Create:

GET /api/ivx/registration/health

Return:

- auth configuration available;
- Supabase reachable;
- members table reachable;
- deals table reachable;
- email configuration status;
- registration service status.

Do not return secret values.

==================================================
20. CANARY REGISTRATION
==================================================

After every deployment, run one safe canary registration in a test namespace.

Verify:

- Auth user;
- member row;
- opportunity-interest row;
- session or email-confirmation path;
- cleanup.

Do not use a real investor email.

Do not leave test accounts mixed with production members.

==================================================
21. CHAOS TESTS
==================================================

Test deliberately:

- Supabase temporarily unavailable;
- member insert timeout;
- interest insert timeout;
- duplicate response;
- network disconnect after Auth creation;
- browser refresh during profile creation;
- stale JavaScript bundle;
- missing global configuration;
- wrong project reference;
- delayed CloudFront cache;
- API returns malformed JSON;
- email confirmation delayed;
- rate limit reached.

Expected:

- no page crash;
- no duplicate user;
- no lost opportunity context;
- clear recovery path;
- trace ID recorded.

==================================================
22. CROSS-PLATFORM TESTS
==================================================

Run on:

- Samsung Browser;
- Android Chrome;
- iPhone Safari;
- desktop Chrome;
- desktop Safari;
- mobile web embedded browser;
- IVX Expo app if the flow is shared.

Test:

- slow 4G;
- Wi-Fi loss;
- browser background;
- device rotation;
- keyboard open;
- browser refresh;
- back button;
- duplicate tap;
- password manager autofill.

==================================================
23. REGRESSION TESTS
==================================================

Add tests for:

1. Valid registration.
2. Existing email.
3. Weak password.
4. Missing config.
5. Supabase client initialization failure.
6. Auth succeeds/profile fails.
7. Profile succeeds/interest fails.
8. Retry resumes correctly.
9. Duplicate submission creates one user.
10. Browser refresh resumes.
11. Expired session.
12. Email confirmation required.
13. Email confirmation disabled.
14. Rate limit.
15. Timeout after Auth creation.
16. Correct error mapping.
17. Password absent from logs.
18. Service-role key absent from browser.
19. Role cannot be escalated by client.
20. Step 4 context preserved.

==================================================
24. RELEASE GATES
==================================================

Do not deploy registration changes unless:

[ ] configuration validation passes;
[ ] all registration tests pass;
[ ] no password appears in logs;
[ ] no service-role key exists in client bundle;
[ ] idempotency test passes;
[ ] partial-recovery tests pass;
[ ] live canary succeeds;
[ ] duplicate count remains zero;
[ ] landing and backend commits match;
[ ] CloudFront serves the new files;
[ ] live mobile browser registration succeeds.

==================================================
FINAL REQUIRED REPORT
==================================================

CONFIGURATION:
Canonical variables:
Production project:
Frontend/backend match:

REGISTRATION:
